\import{fnip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fnip}
\taxon{Definition}
\title{Bifurcation, codimension, and normal form}
\date{2026-11-26}

\p{A homeomorphism of the state space carries
\vocabk{orbits}{fnip-0015} of one continuous-time system onto
orbits of another and preserves the direction of time. That
relation is \newvocab{topological equivalence}.
\citet{Definition 2.1, pp. 39–40}{kuznetsov2004elements} is this
relation. The homeomorphism on those pages is a continuous
invertible map whose inverse is continuous.}

\p{A \newvocab{bifurcation} is the appearance of a
\vocabk{phase portrait}{fnip-0015} that is topologically
nonequivalent to the original one, as the parameters vary.
\citet{Definition 2.11, p. 57}{kuznetsov2004elements} is this
appearance. The continuous-time system on that page is
##{\dot x=f(x,\alpha),}
with #{x\in\mathbb{R}^{n}} and #{\alpha\in\mathbb{R}^{m}}.}

\p{The \newvocab{codimension} of a bifurcation is the dimension of
the parameter space minus the dimension of the bifurcation
boundary. The same number is the number of independent conditions
that determine the bifurcation.
\citet{Definition 2.13, p. 62}{kuznetsov2004elements} is this
number.}

\p{Two systems that depend on parameters are locally topologically
equivalent near the origin when a map
#{(x,\alpha)\mapsto(h_{\alpha}(x),p(\alpha))} is defined near
#{(x,\alpha)=(0,0)}. Here #{p} is a homeomorphism of a neighborhood
of #{\alpha=0} with #{p(0)=0}. Each #{h_{\alpha}} is a
homeomorphism of a neighborhood of the origin, with
#{h_{0}(0)=0}. It carries orbits in that neighborhood onto orbits
at #{p(\alpha)} and preserves the direction of time.
\citet{Definition 2.15, pp. 63–64}{kuznetsov2004elements} is this
relation. The homeomorphism of the state space, defined above,
compares two phase portraits at fixed parameters.}

\p{A polynomial system
##{\dot\xi=g(\xi,\beta;\sigma)}
is a \newvocab{topological normal form} for a bifurcation when
every generic system with the equilibrium at the origin that
satisfies the same bifurcation conditions at the critical
parameter is locally topologically equivalent near the origin, in
the sense just stated, to that polynomial system
for some coefficients #{\sigma}.
\citet{Definition 2.16, p. 65}{kuznetsov2004elements} is this form.
On that page a generic system satisfies finitely many inequalities
#{N_{i}[f]\ne 0}. The conditions written with derivatives in the
state, at the critical parameter, are the nondegeneracy
conditions. The conditions written with derivatives in the
parameters are the transversality conditions.}
